import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Step-Arrow Concurrency: Handl-Authored Scheduler over epoll","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Step-Arrow Concurrency: Handl-Authored Scheduler over epoll","type":"adr","date":"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z","status":"accepted","status_note":"accepted"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"web/project/adrs/0122-step-arrow-concurrency.md","filePath":"vendored/web/project/adrs/0122-step-arrow-concurrency.md"}`),a={name:`web/project/adrs/0122-step-arrow-concurrency.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="step-arrow-concurrency-handl-authored-scheduler-over-epoll" tabindex="-1">Step-Arrow Concurrency: Handl-Authored Scheduler over epoll <a class="header-anchor" href="#step-arrow-concurrency-handl-authored-scheduler-over-epoll" aria-label="Permalink to “Step-Arrow Concurrency: Handl-Authored Scheduler over epoll”">​</a></h1><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>A native handl HTTP server needs concurrency, but neither exec engine can suspend a running computation (<code>plugin-engine-exec-bytecode</code> and <code>plugin-engine-exec-tree-walk</code> have no <code>Suspend</code>/<code>Resume</code>; the only &quot;step&quot; machinery is the ArrowLoop <code>{state}</code>/<code>{out}</code> protocol). <code>handle</code>/<code>op</code> dispatch is synchronous — the &quot;rest of the body&quot; lives in pending Rust eval frames, so it cannot be captured as a continuation.</p><p>The constraints: no Rust concurrency/scheduling code (user requirement), and per ADR-0072 the non-determinism should be externalized into a scheduler over an event stream. libc bindings for sockets/epoll/fcntl/errno already exist (<code>handl-packages/generated/libc-c-linux-x86_64/</code>).</p><p>Options considered:</p><ul><li><strong>Rust backend</strong> (<code>plugin-lib-concurrent</code>, threads + mpsc): fastest, but violates the no-Rust-scheduling constraint and ADR-0083&#39;s direction.</li><li><strong>VM continuations</strong> (suspend/resume instrs): real <code>await</code> ergonomics, but kernel surgery; defers everything.</li><li><strong>libuv/libevent</strong>: callback-based C loops need reverse FFI trampolines that do not exist; a poll-ified C shim re-exposes epoll with extra vendored code — the &quot;intermediate layer&quot; rejected by ADR-0083.</li><li><strong>Step arrows over epoll</strong>: tasks are explicit state machines the scheduler round-robins. No new infra; epoll bindings already generated.</li></ul><p>Sanada&#39;s <em>Algebraic Effects and Handlers for Arrows</em> (the calculus&#39;s foundation) makes the step model principled rather than ad hoc: folding a task arrow into the free A-algebra yields <code>{op, args, k}</code> nodes where <code>k</code> is the continuation <em>as data</em> — serializable, inspectable, schedulable. The explicit <code>{state}</code> field is the same defunctionalized continuation written by hand.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p>Adopt the <strong>step-arrow model</strong>, staged:</p><ul><li><strong>B0 (now)</strong>: <code>handl-packages/concurrent/concurrent.hnl</code> defines the step protocol — a task is <code>S ~&gt; Step</code> where <code>Step = {state: S} | {out: V} | {call: Req, state: S}</code>; <code>{call}</code> suspends on a named op (<code>name</code> is the discriminator — <code>op</code> is a grammar keyword) and the scheduler resumes the task by re-invoking the arrow with <code>state.result = R</code>. <code>handl-packages/concurrent-epoll/</code> implements it over the generated libc bindings: run queue, spawn/await/<code>enter</code> continuation frames, rendezvous + bounded channels with select, mutex/rwlock/semaphore/ atomics, cancellation tokens, monotonic timers, nonblocking recv/send/accept/connect_wait/close with epoll parking, EBADF wake-on-close, and deadlock reporting. Epoll is created lazily so CPU-only workloads never touch it.</li><li><strong>B1 (later)</strong>: a free-algebra interpreter (à la <code>arrow_model.hnl</code>) that suspends ordinary arrow code for the user — straight-line <code>await</code> ergonomics on the same wire protocol, no VM surgery.</li></ul><p>No new Rust. HTTP/RFC packages build on this once the backend is verified.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Users write phase-tagged state machines today (<code>s.phase</code> dispatch); the ergonomics cost is accepted pending B1.</li><li><code>concurrent-epoll</code> is Linux-only; a kqueue/IOCP backend would be a sibling package under the same <code>concurrent</code> contract.</li><li><code>() ~[1]~&gt;</code> evaluates its body at module load (it is an application, not a binding) — nullary helpers must take a dummy parameter.</li><li>Runtime verification of the backend waits on the in-flight import/eval refactor landing (imported exported arrows must be applicable; <code>ffi.call</code> must dispatch through the FFI layer).</li></ul>`,13)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};